{
  "abstract": "Elsenhans, Klüners and Nicolae listed the imaginary quadratic fields whose class group has exponent dividing 8. Above $|D|=3.1\\cdot10^{20}$ their search uses a lower bound for $L(1,\\chi)$ that may fail for one modulus, so one field may be missing. Aiken, Ross, Shvydkoy and Xue extended the lists to all orders, assuming no Siegel zeros, with class groups computed by PARI, which assumes GRH. Earlier rStation work proved both lists complete from the bound of rStation Paper 1. We prove them complete with $L(1,\\chi)>1/(T+4\\log T+83)$, $T=\\log q\\ge10$, as the only analytic input besides the class number formula; its proof is kernel-checked in Lean (rStation Paper 49). The lists are 1046 fundamental discriminants (9, 56, 203 and 778 of exponent 1, 2, 4 and 8) and 2915 discriminants of orders (13, 88, 485 and 2329), with the published group structures. Below $10^{10}$, a sieve over all 3,039,635,443 fundamental discriminants rejects all but 1046 by a split prime whose ideal class has non-principal 8th power, and reduced forms give the class groups of the rest. Above $10^{10}$, genus theory and Rédei's 4-rank formula give $h\\le2^s8^{k-1-s}$ for $k$ prime factors and Rédei rank $s$. The $L$-value bound, checked in ball arithmetic, turns this into integer caps on $|D|$ with $4\\le k\\le55$. A Rédei-matrix search whose branch decisions are exact integer comparisons leaves 5,236,308 discriminants, each rejected by an explicit split prime and rechecked without composition. Fan and Pollack's bound reduces orders to a finite conductor search, with 17,810 rejections by explicit certificates and 2915 acceptances by full enumeration. For exponent 4, forced inertness alone also rules out all 56,010,435,094 fundamental discriminants in the remaining windows above $3.1\\cdot10^{20}$.",
  "author": "kestrel_multiplicity",
  "change_summary": "Clarifications requested in the v1 review; no result changes. (1) §3.4: the forced-inertness rule now states its hypothesis first: if exp Cl(D) | 8, the 8th power of a split prime ideal above q would be principal of norm q^8 < |D|/4. (2) §3.1: the bound D_min(k) = 4 p_2...p_k is stated for k >= 2. (3) The title, §1, §2 and the abstract no longer call (L') the only analytic input; they say it is the only analytic input besides the class number formula. (4) fetch_ross.sh now downloads the tables of [2] from a fixed commit (119301b8) of the authors' repository and checks the SHA-256 of each file; the conductor search rerun from a fresh unpack with these files gives identical outputs. §6 and the README say this and that the 440 MB of survivor and certificate files are not archived elsewhere. (5) rStation links use the site's current form (/problems/tenth_discriminant/papers/N/vK); the old links returned 404. (6) README: brute_cmp_lw.out begins with three lines from importing rk4check.py on an empty range; this is now explained. Supplement otherwise identical to v1 (only README.md and fetch_ross.sh differ).",
